[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Django Test Cases: TestCase vs TransactionTestCase vs LiveServerTestCase"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Django provides a robust testing framework built on top Python's built-in unittest library. When writing integration and database tests, choosing the correct test case class is critical for performance, data isolation, and feature compatibility. This tutorial covers TestCase, TransactionTestCase, and LiveServerTestCase, explaining when and why to use each."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Django TestCase: The Default Choice"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"TestCase is the most commonly used test class in Django. It is designed to run tests inside a database transaction to ensure test isolation. After each test method executes, Django rolls back the transaction, leaving the database in its initial clean state."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Wraps each test method in a database transaction and rolls it back upon completion."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extremely fast because it avoids dropping and recreating database tables."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cannot test code that relies on explicit database transactions (like atomic blocks with commit hooks) or multi-threaded environments where connections differ."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"from django.test import TestCase
from myapp.models import Article

class ArticleTestCase(TestCase):
    def setUp(self):
        Article.objects.create(title="Test Article", content="Hello World")

    def test_article_creation(self):
        article = Article.objects.get(title="Test Article")
        self.assertEqual(article.content, "Hello World")"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Django TransactionTestCase: For Complex Database Operations"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"TransactionTestCase does not wrap tests in a database transaction. Instead, it flushes the database (or truncates tables depending on the backend) after each test. Use this when your code explicitly handles database transactions, uses database features like savepoints across connections, or tests consumer code that triggers asynchronous tasks outside the transaction boundary."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Does not use database rollbacks; flushes/truncates data instead."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Slower than TestCase due to table truncation overhead."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Required when testing transaction.on_commit() hooks or complex isolation levels."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"from django.test import TransactionTestCase
from django.db import transaction
from myapp.models import Wallet

class WalletTransactionTestCase(TransactionTestCase):
    reset_sequences = True

    def test_atomic_transaction(self):
        with transaction.atomic():
            wallet = Wallet.objects.create(balance=100)
        self.assertEqual(wallet.balance, 100)"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Django LiveServerTestCase: For End-to-End Browser Testing"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"LiveServerTestCase extends TransactionTestCase by launching a live background Django server in a separate thread. This is essential when writing end-to-end (E2E) tests using tools like Selenium, Playwright, or Cypress, where an actual HTTP server running on a local port is required to process requests."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spins up a live development server on a background thread during test execution."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides the self.live_server_url attribute to easily target endpoints."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inherits from TransactionTestCase, meaning it truncates tables rather than rolling back transactions."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"from django.test import LiveServerTestCase
from urllib.request import urlopen

class MyEndToEndTestCase(LiveServerTestCase):
    def test_homepage_response(self):
        response = urlopen(self.live_server_url + "/")
        self.assertEqual(response.status, 200)"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default to TestCase for 90 percent of your models, views, and form tests due to speed."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Switch to TransactionTestCase only when testing explicit database transactions or multi-threading behavior."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use LiveServerTestCase exclusively for browser-based integration tests requiring a real HTTP server."}]
